   2: 
   3: THE MECHANISM THIS DETECTS, WHICH IS OUR MOST RECURRENT ONE
   4:     A CHECK THAT REPORTS SUCCESS WITHOUT HAVING PERFORMED THE CHECK.
   5: 
   6:     Five independent instances, all found in this project:
   7:       1. The pre-push hook printed "Regression check PASS" while `$?` after a
   8:          pipeline read `tail`, so the failure branch was unreachable. Every push
   9:          from that clone was ungated while displaying a green result. 6 of 12
  10:          checkouts still carried it today.
  11:       2. figure_audit passed on pages it could not render -- a hidden or 0x0
  12:          element measured as a silent zero rather than reported unmeasurable.
  13:       3. A source leg returned a clean verdict having read nothing, because a
  14:          corpus-reachable gate handed back our own just-written text.
  15:       4. CHK005's mutation test swept one key and reported the estimate protected;
  16:          the real pooled value was never mutated, so it could not have failed.
  17:       5. The Word-vs-HTML alignment gate compared only the sections BOTH surfaces
  18:          emit, so a section present in one and absent from the other was silently
  19:          OUT OF SCOPE rather than a divergence. The extraction provenance table
  20:          was missing from every Word manuscript this project ever produced and
  21:          nothing could have reported it. A GATE THAT COMPARES ONLY WHAT BOTH
  22:          SURFACES HAVE CAN NEVER DETECT ABSENCE -- the intersection is not the
  23:          expected set, and using it as one converts every missing section into a
  24:          pass. The fix is an expected-section manifest projected from the object,
  25:          so absence FAILS instead of falling outside the comparison.
  26: 
  27:     THE COMPANION RULE, AND THE ONE THIS PROJECT HAS HAD TO RE-LEARN FOUR TIMES:
  28: 
  29:         A VERDICT THAT MEANS "I COULD NOT CHECK" IS NEVER A PASS.
  30:         NOT_APPLICABLE IS NEVER A PASS.
  31:         UNCHECKABLE IS NEVER A PASS.
  32:         UNMEASURED IS NEVER A PASS.
  33:         AND NONE OF THEM MAY BE COUNTED INTO A CLEAN DENOMINATOR.
  34: 
  35:     This is stated HERE, once, because it has been restated locally in four gates
  36:     and four copies of one rule drift apart. Each of the four earned it against a
  37:     real artefact:
  38: 
  39:       card_alignment_gate      an audit-first card is UNCHECKABLE by construction.
  40:                                Folding those into the pass count reported "0.0%
  41:                                drift" over 1.2% of the corpus -- a reassuring
  42:                                headline computed over the sliver that could be
  43:                                read, with the enormous unmeasured remainder
  44:                                presented as if it were not there.
  45:       declared_contrast_gate   a two-arm registration returns NOT_APPLICABLE. Only
  46:                                one contrast is possible, so the trial can neither
  47:                                exhibit the defect nor clear itself of it. Counting
  48:                                those as passes would let a corpus of two-arm trials
  49:                                report 100% clean while establishing nothing.
  50:       arm_identity_gate        arm labels that identify nothing ('1' vs '2') are
  51:                                UNCHECKABLE. The gate that returned PASS on finding
  52:                                nothing to inspect is how eleven properties were
  53:                                once claimed where four were established.
  54:       estimand_definition_gate a definition containing an unrecognised event term
  55:                                is UNCHECKABLE and OUTRANKS both PASS and WITHDRAWN,
  56:                                because a partial reading that reports agreement is
  57:                                the CKD false agreement.
  58: 
  59:     A SECOND RULE OF THE SAME FAMILY, EARNED 2026-08-18:
  60: 
  61:         A HEADLINE IS A POINT **AND** AN INTERVAL. A REPRODUCTION THAT MATCHES THE
  62:         POINT AND MISSES THE INTERVAL IS NOT A REPRODUCTION.
  63: 
  64:     headline_reproducible_gate matched the point alone in its first cut and
  65:     reported COLCHICINE_CVD as REPRODUCED: fixed-effect over two of its trials
  66:     gives 0.7506 against a published 0.75, and THAT combination's interval is
  67:     0.6512 to 0.8652 against a published 0.61 to 0.91. Two digits agreeing is a
  68:     coincidence, not a derivation, and calling it a reproduction would have cleared
  69:     a page whose interval follows from nothing.
  70: 
  71:     This is the same shape as agreement not authenticating anything: matching on
  72:     the cheap half of a claim says nothing about the half that carries the
  73:     uncertainty. Where a check compares a composite quantity, it must compare ALL
  74:     of it or name which part it compared. POINT_ONLY exists as a distinct verdict
  75:     for exactly that reason.
  76: 
  77:     THE TEST FOR WHETHER A VERDICT IS BEING MISCOUNTED: ask what the check would
  78:     report over a corpus in which it can see NOTHING AT ALL. If the answer is
  79:     anything other than "nothing was established", the not-a-pass verdicts are
  80:     leaking into the pass count.
  81: 
  82:     AND A PROPORTION MUST CARRY ITS COMPARABLE FRACTION INLINE OR REFUSE TO
  83:     RENDER. "0.0% drift" and "0.0% drift over 6 of 514 cards" are different
  84:     claims, and only the second one is true.
  85: 
  86:     The shape is identical in all five: the SUCCESS PATH IS REACHABLE AND THE
  87:     FAILURE PATH IS NOT. A green result is therefore evidence of nothing, and
  88:     nobody investigates a green result -- which is why these survive.
  89: 
  90:     SIXTH INSTANCE, AND THE FIRST ON THE MEASURING SIDE RATHER THAN THE CHECKING
  91:     SIDE. A lane nearly reported the extraction table as MISSING from a page that
  92:     has it: its text extractor returned 0 occurrences of "Sources" in 101,864
  93:     characters, and was wrong. The instrument silently omitted content. It caught
  94:     itself only because a second read of the raw HTML contradicted it.
  95: 
  96:     A MEASUREMENT INSTRUMENT THAT SILENTLY OMITS CONTENT IS THE SAME MECHANISM AS
  97:     A GATE PASSING HAVING READ NOTHING. In both, the failure is invisible because
  98:     the output is well formed: "0 occurrences" and "PASS" are equally ordinary
  99:     results, and neither carries any signal that the input was incomplete.
 100: 
 101:     SO THE RULE IS: any page-content check must read RAW HTML, never rendered-text
 102:     extraction. But the rule has a second half, and missing it produced a wrong
 103:     number within the hour of adopting the first half:
 104: 
 105:         RAW HTML DOES NOT MISS HIDDEN CONTENT. IT OVER-COUNTS SOURCE AS CONTENT.
 106: 
 107:     A census of resolvable source links over raw HTML returned "62.8% of pages
 108:     carry a registry or PubMed link". They do not. The matches were JavaScript
 109:     TEMPLATE SOURCE inside <script> -- href="https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/
 110:     ${escapeHtml(t.id)}" -- counted as links because a raw-HTML regex cannot tell
 111:     a string literal in a program from an attribute in a document. Recomputed with
 112:     <script>/<style> BODIES dropped and ${...} placeholders excluded, the figure
 113:     was 2.2%.
 114: 
 115:     LOGGED AS A RULE REGRESSION, NOT JUST A MISTAKE. "Read raw HTML, never
 116:     rendered-text extraction" was ADOPTED AS A RULE in response to the
 117:     under-counting instance, and within the hour it made a measurement WORSE than
 118:     no rule at all: 62.8% against a true 2.2%, a factor of forty, AND IT ERRED IN
 119:     THE REASSURING DIRECTION -- it said the corpus was well sourced when it is
 120:     not. A rule that fails toward comfort will not be questioned, because its
 121:     output is the answer everyone hoped for.
 122: 
 123:     So rules earn the same treatment as gates: state what a rule does NOT
 124:     establish at the moment it is adopted, and re-derive one number under the new
 125:     rule and the old one before trusting it. The half-rule was correct about what
 126:     it was reacting to and silently wrong about everything else, which is the
 127:     normal shape of a rule written from a single incident.
 128: 
 129:     Neither instrument alone is correct:
 130:         rendered text   omits what is not displayed   -> UNDER-counts
 131:         raw HTML        includes what is not content  -> OVER-counts
 132:     Read raw HTML so nothing hidden is lost, then ATTRIBUTE CONTENT TO ITS
 133:     CONTEXT: script and style bodies are program text, not page content. Note
 134:     this is the same correction already applied to index_markup_gate, which on
 135:     its first run flagged three anchors inside a script comment -- the corpus has
 136:     now taught this lesson twice in one day, in opposite directions.
 137: 
 138:     AND WHEN A PAGE BUILDS ITS OWN DOM, SAY WHICH ONE YOU MEASURED. "In the
 139:     served markup" and "after JavaScript runs" are different claims about
 140:     different readers. On this corpus they differ by a factor of forty: 2.2% of
 141:     pages carry a resolvable registry link in the markup, while 27 of 31 sampled
 142:     pages carry one once the page has run. Reporting either alone, without
 143:     naming which, is a true sentence that misleads.
 144: 
 145: THE MATCHED PAIR -- THE CLEANEST EVIDENCE IN THIS FILE
 146:     Two probes, one root cause, opposite failure directions, wildly different
 147:     survival times. This is the selection principle demonstrated rather than
 148:     argued.
 149: 
 150:     ROOT CAUSE, both cases: A PROBE THAT ESTABLISHES SOMETHING ANSWERS, NEVER
 151:     THAT THE RIGHT THING ANSWERS.
 152: 
 153:     FALSE DEATH -- the agy liveness probe queried the wrong model pool and
 154:       reported a seat dead that was fully alive. Cost: an entire verification
 155:       family stood down. CAUGHT THE SAME DAY, because "the seat is dead" is an
 156:       alarming result and somebody immediately went and looked.
 157: 
 158:     FALSE LIFE -- the regression gate probed `curl -sf .../index.html` on port
 159:       8787 and reused whatever answered. What answered was a SIBLING WORKING TREE
 160:       of the same repository on a different branch. The gate loaded, measured and
 161:       PASSED pages that were not the pages being pushed -- ARNI at 912,140 bytes
 162:       over the wire against 6,147,695 on disk. SURVIVED ALL DAY, and every green
 163:       it produced was relayed upward as confidence, because "regression check
 164:       PASS" is exactly what everyone wanted to read.
 165: 
 166:     Same defect class. One was caught in hours, the other ran a full day feeding
 167:     false assurance into reports, AND THE ONLY DIFFERENCE WAS WHICH DIRECTION IT
 168:     FAILED IN. Nothing about the false-life version was subtler or better hidden.
 169:     It simply never gave anyone a reason to look.
 170: 
 171:     Note also that clones make hash- and size-comparison useless as an identity
 172:     check here: two working trees of one repository agree on most files most of
 173:     the time, so any comparison of EXISTING content passes constantly. The only
 174:     probe another directory cannot satisfy is a NONCE -- content that did not
 175:     exist anywhere a moment ago. And the response to a failed identity check is
 176:     REFUSE (exit 2), not warn: a gate reading another directory is worse than no
 177:     gate, because no gate at least never produces a green.
 178: 
 179: THE PAYOFF CASE FOR "A GATE NEEDS A CONSTRUCTIBLE FAILING INPUT"
 180:     The wrong-tree defect was not found by looking for it. It was found because
 181:     the three-state verdict needed a fixture that could FAIL, the real pages
 182:     could not produce one (the double-load warms the cache, so they always
 183:     passed), and a purpose-built fixture therefore had to be written -- which
 184:     then 404'd against a server that was returning 200 for every real page.
 185: 
 186:     THE DISCIPLINE DID NOT MERELY VERIFY THE CHECK IT WAS APPLIED TO. IT
 187:     DISCOVERED AN UNRELATED AND MUCH LARGER DEFECT. That is the argument for the
 188:     standard, and it belongs here rather than in a footnote: insisting on a
 189:     constructible failure forces you to interact with the system in a way that
 190:     passing tests never do, and the discrepancies you trip over on the way are
 191:     frequently worth more than the check you set out to build.
 192: 
 193: THE OTHER META-MECHANISM -- THE ACTION SUCCEEDED, THE EFFECT WAS NEVER CONFIRMED
 194:     Four instances, and it may be the most productive pattern in this file:
 195: 
 196:         PUSH IS NOT DEPLOY               the ref moved; the site served the old bytes
 197:         THE REPAIR EXISTING IS NOT THE REPAIR ARRIVING
 198:                                          the hook was fixed; 6 of 12 checkouts still ran the old one
 199:         A LIBRARY NO BUILD INVOKES CATCHES NOTHING
 200:                                          the detector was correct and was never called
 201:         WRITING AN ARTEFACT IS NOT PRESERVING IT
 202:                                          the waiver register was written into gitignored outputs/;
 203:                                          it wrote fine, read back fine, and existed in no clone
 204: 
 205:     In every one the actor had every reason to believe it had worked, BECAUSE THE
 206:     ACTION ITSELF SUCCEEDED AND REPORTED SUCCESS. `git push` exits 0. The editor
 207:     saves. `open(path, "w")` returns. Nothing anywhere is lying; the confirmation
 208:     simply answers a different question from the one that matters.
 209: 
 210:     THE QUESTION IS NEVER "DID I DO IT". IT IS "DID IT LAND WHERE IT HAS TO BE."
 211:     Those come apart whenever an artefact must cross a boundary to take effect --
 212:     a deploy, a clone, a working tree, an import. Every such boundary needs its
 213:     own confirmation, taken FROM THE FAR SIDE: fetch the served bytes, run the
 214:     hook from a second checkout, assert the detector was invoked, ask git whether
 215:     the file is tracked.
 216: 
 217:     And note the direction, which is why all four survived: EVERY ONE FAILS
 218:     TOWARD COMFORT. An untracked backlog does not error, it quietly stops
 219:     existing, and the corpus then looks better than it is.
 220: 
 221: THE MECHANISM BEHIND THE MECHANISM -- WHICH DIRECTION DOES THE FAILURE POINT?
 222:     Ask of every check, gate, rule and instrument: WHEN THIS IS WRONG, IS ITS
 223:     OUTPUT ALARMING OR REASSURING?
 224: 
 225:         Fails toward ALARM   -> someone investigates -> it gets fixed or removed.
 226:                                 It cannot survive long, because a false alarm is
 227:                                 expensive to whoever is standing next to it.
 228:         Fails toward COMFORT -> nobody investigates a green result -> it survives
 229:                                 indefinitely, and its survival is mistaken for
 230:                                 its being correct.
 231: 
 232:     EVERY MECHANISM FOUND IN THIS PROJECT FAILED TOWARD COMFORT. That is not
 233:     coincidence, it is SELECTION. The alarming ones were all removed years ago by
 234:     whoever got tired of them; what remains in any long-lived codebase is
 235:     disproportionately the set of checks that are wrong in the pleasant direction.
 236: 
 237:       - the pre-push hook that printed PASS because $? read `tail`   -> comfort
 238:       - figure_audit scoring an unrenderable element as zero         -> comfort
 239:       - a source leg verifying against our own just-written text     -> comfort
 240:       - a mutation test that never mutated the value it protects     -> comfort
 241:       - an alignment gate comparing only the intersection, so every
 242:         MISSING section was out of scope rather than a divergence    -> comfort
 243:       - a text extractor silently dropping content: "0 occurrences"  -> comfort
 244:       - "read raw HTML", which counted <script> template source and
 245:         reported 62.8% sourced against a true 2.2%                   -> comfort
 246: 
 247:     Seven mechanisms, seven in the same direction, zero in the other. A set that
 248:     lopsided is not a sample of our mistakes -- it is a sample of the mistakes
 249:     that were ABLE TO LAST.
 250: 
 251:     PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE, and the reason this is written here rather than
 252:     admired: comfortable failure modes cannot be found by waiting for them to
 253:     cause trouble, because not causing trouble is precisely their property. They
 254:     have to be hunted deliberately. So when adding any check, write down its
 255:     comfortable failure mode FIRST -- the specific way it could report success
 256:     having established nothing -- and then construct that input and confirm it
 257:     blocks. If you cannot construct it, you have not understood the check yet.
 258: 
 259:     And prefer designs where the comfortable direction is unavailable: a manifest
 260:     of EXPECTED sections rather than a comparison of shared ones; UNMEASURED as a
 261:     distinct verdict from PASS; an unloadable page recorded as unmeasured rather
 262:     than as zero links. In each case the fix was not a better check but a shape in
 263:     which silence cannot be read as success.
 264: 
 265: PROMOTION CRITERION -- REPLAY A REAL PAST DEFECT
 266:     A synthetic failing input proves a detector CAN fire. It does not prove the
 267:     detector DISCRIMINATES: a rule that fires on everything also passes that test.
 268:     Where a real past defect exists, replay it, and require the detector to fire on
 269:     it AND stay silent on the parts that were genuinely fine.
 270: 
 271:     Worked example: the section-manifest gate was run against ARNI's pre-port
 272:     manuscript docmodel taken from git. It fired on extraction_provenance -- the
 273:     one section actually missing -- and passed certainty, pooled_result,
 274:     published_comparison, risk_of_bias and screening, all five of which were fine.
 275:     A synthetic deletion could not have established that.
 276: 
 277:     Free discriminating cases already sitting in this repo's history, most of which
 278:     no detector has yet been run against:
 279:       - the inverted provenance conditional in build_app_v2
 280:       - the hardcoded _DOCMODEL that put ARNI's manuscript on four other pages
 281:       - the ordinal-split parser that merged two screening records into one
 282:       - the unicode-minus comparison that made -71.31 read as +71.31
 283:       - the substring classifier matching AF_ inside TAF_TDF, TAVI inside roTAVIrus
 284:       - the citation-year "correction" that changed a value to make a test pass
 285:     A detector promoted without being run against at least one of these has been
 286:     tested only against its author's imagination.
 287: 
 288: THE GENERAL DETECTOR
 289:     For every gate, ask: WHAT INPUT WOULD MAKE THIS FAIL? Construct it and show it
 290:     failing. A gate with no constructible failing input is not a gate. That single
 291:     question would have caught all five.
 292: 
 293: WHAT THIS SCRIPT CHECKS MECHANICALLY
 294:     D1 PIPELINE STATUS   `$?` read immediately after a pipeline. Generic: greps
 295:                          every hook and shell script. One line, and it would have
 296:                          caught the hook.
 297:     D2 SCOPE HONESTY     a gate's claimed scope against what it actually globs.
 298:                          The hook's header said "53 apps ... ~60 seconds" while
 299:                          its script globbed 1,449 pages. The false claim is what
 300:                          made bypassing feel reasonable.
 301:     D3 SKIP FLAGS        an advertised escape hatch. A gate that documents its own
 302:                          bypass will be bypassed.
 303:     D4 NO SYS.EXIT       a Python gate that never exits non-zero cannot block.
 304: 
 305: WHAT A FULL PASS DOES NOT ESTABLISH -- written in advance
 306:     - NOT that the gate's LOGIC is correct, only that it is capable of failing and
 307:       honest about its scope. A gate that can fail and checks the wrong thing
 308:       passes here.
 309:     - NOT that the gate is wired up. core.hooksPath unset, or set to a directory
 310:       with no hook file, are separate masks -- both seen today -- and are checked
 311:       by the clone sweep, not by this.
 312:     - NOT that a constructible failing input EXISTS. That question is the real
 313:       detector and it needs a human; this catches four mechanical proxies for it.
 314: """
 315: from __future__ import annotations
 316: import os, re, sys, io, glob
 317: 
 318: if __name__ == "__main__":
 319:     sys.stdout = io.TextIOWrapper(sys.stdout.buffer, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
 320: 
 321: PIPE_STATUS = re.compile(r"\|[^\n|]*\n\s*(?:STATUS|RC|rc|status)\s*=\s*\$\?")
 322: BARE_AFTER_PIPE = re.compile(r"^[^#\n]*\|[^\n]*\n\s*\w+=\$\?", re.M)
 323: SCOPE_CLAIM = re.compile(r"(\d[\d,]{1,6})\s*(?:-|\s)?apps?\b", re.I)
 324: GLOB_CALL = re.compile(r"glob\(\s*['\"]([^'\"]+)['\"]")
 325: SKIP = re.compile(r"^\s*if\s*\[\s*\"?\$\{?(SKIP|BYPASS)[A-Z_]*\}?\"?\s*=", re.M)
 326: 
 327: 
 328: def check_shell(path, text):
 329:     out = []
 330:     if BARE_AFTER_PIPE.search(text):
 331:         out.append(("D1_PIPELINE_STATUS",
 332:                     "reads $? immediately after a pipeline: that is the LAST stage's "
 333:                     "status, so the failure branch is unreachable"))
 334:     if SKIP.search(text):
 335:         out.append(("D3_SKIP_FLAG", "advertises an environment-variable bypass"))
 336:     return out
 337: 
 338: 
 339: def check_scope(hook_text, script_path):
 340:     """Claimed app count in the hook header vs what the script really globs.
 341: 
 342:     A repaired gate that DOCUMENTS the old false claim must not be flagged for
 343:     quoting it. The first sweep fired on the fixed hook because its header
 344:     explains "the header claimed 53 apps" as part of the repair note - the
 345:     detector was reading a description of the defect as the defect. Any scope
 346:     claim inside a historical block is skipped.
 347:     """
 348:     if re.search(r"WHY THIS FILE WAS REWRITTEN|previous version|was rewritten|"
 349:                  r"the header claimed", hook_text, re.I):
 350:         return []
 351:     m = SCOPE_CLAIM.search(hook_text)
 352:     if not m or not script_path or not os.path.exists(script_path):
 353:         return []
 354:     claimed = int(m.group(1).replace(",", ""))
 355:     st = open(script_path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 356:     g = GLOB_CALL.search(st)
 357:     if not g:
 358:         return []
 359:     root = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(script_path)))
 360:     actual = len(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, g.group(1))))
 361:     if actual and abs(actual - claimed) > max(5, 0.25 * claimed):
 362:         return [("D2_SCOPE_DISHONEST",
 363:                  "header claims %d apps; the script globs %r and matches %d -- a "
 364:                  "factor of %.1f" % (claimed, g.group(1), actual, actual / max(claimed, 1)))]
 365:     return []
 366: 
 367: 
 368: def check_python_gate(path, text):
 369:     if "sys.exit" not in text and "raise SystemExit" not in text:
 370:         return [("D4_NO_EXIT", "no sys.exit anywhere: this gate cannot return non-zero")]
 371:     return []
 372: 
 373: 
 374: def scan(roots):
 375:     findings = []
 376:     for root in roots:
 377:         for hook in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, ".githooks", "*")) + \
 378:                     glob.glob(os.path.join(root, ".git", "hooks", "*")):
 379:             # only real hook files: a README in .githooks is documentation, and
 380:             # flagging it was noise that inflated the first sweep.
 381:             if not os.path.isfile(hook) or hook.endswith((".sample", ".md", ".txt")):
 382:                 continue
 383:             if os.path.basename(hook) not in (
 384:                     "pre-push", "pre-commit", "commit-msg", "pre-receive",
 385:                     "prepare-commit-msg", "post-checkout"):
 386:                 continue
 387:             t = open(hook, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 388:             for c, w in check_shell(hook, t):
 389:                 findings.append((hook, c, w))
 390:             sp = os.path.join(root, "scripts", "regression_check.py")
 391:             for c, w in check_scope(t, sp):
 392:                 findings.append((hook, c, w))
 393:         # D4 applies ONLY to scripts a hook actually invokes. Globbing *gate* and
 394:         # *check* swept in aggregate_multi_agent.py and friends -- reporting tools
 395:         # that were never gates. A detector that flags non-gates for not being
 396:         # gates is measuring the wrong population, and it inflated the first
 397:         # sweep to 217 findings, most of them meaningless.
 398:         invoked = set()
 399:         for hook in glob.glob(os.path.join(root, ".githooks", "pre-*")):
 400:             if os.path.isfile(hook):
 401:                 ht = open(hook, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 402:                 for mm in re.finditer(r"scripts/([A-Za-z0-9_]+\.py)", ht):
 403:                     invoked.add(os.path.join(root, "scripts", mm.group(1)))
 404:         for py in sorted(invoked):
 405:             if not os.path.exists(py):
 406:                 continue
 407:             t = open(py, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 408:             for c, w in check_python_gate(py, t):
 409:                 findings.append((py, c, w))
 410:     return findings
 411: 
 412: 
 413: def selftest() -> int:
 414:     """The broken hook is kept as a fixture. A detector proved only on the fixed
 415:     version is the same defect one level up."""
 416:     ok = True
 417:     broken = r"F:\claude-temp\finerenone-pre-push.BROKEN.bak"
 418:     if os.path.exists(broken):
 419:         t = open(broken, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 420:         hits = {c for c, _ in check_shell(broken, t)}
 421:         p1 = "D1_PIPELINE_STATUS" in hits
 422:         p3 = "D3_SKIP_FLAG" in hits
 423:         print("  POSITIVE the broken hook: D1 pipeline-status %s, D3 skip-flag %s"
 424:               % ("FIRES" if p1 else "SILENT -- WRONG", "FIRES" if p3 else "SILENT -- WRONG"))
 425:         ok &= p1 and p3
 426:     else:
 427:         print("  POSITIVE fixture absent -- NOT PROVEN"); ok = False
 428:     fixed = r"F:\rapidmeta-ssot-shell\.githooks\pre-push"
 429:     if os.path.exists(fixed):
 430:         t = open(fixed, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
 431:         hits = {c for c, _ in check_shell(fixed, t)}
 432:         clean = not hits
 433:         print("  NEGATIVE the repaired hook: %s"
 434:               % ("SILENT" if clean else "FIRES on %s -- WRONG" % sorted(hits)))
 435:         ok &= clean
 436:     print("\nWHAT A FAILURE WOULD LOOK LIKE: the broken hook passing, which is a gate "
 437:           "that cannot fail being certified as a gate.")
 438:     print("-> SELFTEST PASS" if ok else "-> SELFTEST FAILED")
 439:     return 0 if ok else 1
 440: 
 441: 
 442: def main() -> int:
 443:     if "--selftest" in sys.argv:
 444:         return selftest()
 445:     roots = sys.argv[1:] or [r"F:\rapidmeta-ssot-shell", r"F:\rapidmeta-finerenone"]
 446:     f = scan(roots)
 447:     print("gates scanned across %d root(s); findings: %d" % (len(roots), len(f)))
